.TH DOTTORE-SCOPE 5 "2026" "Il Dottore" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
dottore-scope, dottore-target, dottore-fleet \- Il Dottore configuration files
.SH DESCRIPTION
Il Dottore reads three YAML files. The
.I scope
authorizes a scan; the
.I target
describes what is scanned; the
.I fleet
declares many targets at once. Secrets are never written into any of them: keys are
referenced by environment variable and resolved only at send time. A key written twice in one
mapping is refused in all three, with where it was first written and where again, instead of
keeping the last value. Keys brought in by a << merge can still be overridden; two << in one
mapping are refused too, so merge several maps with one list (<<: [*a, *b]).
.SH "SCOPE FILE"
The authorization record. No scanning command runs without it. Default-deny: any host or
path not under a target's endpoints is never contacted. An optional top-level checksum
(sha256 of the body, the checksum line excluded) is verified when present, so a scope edited
without updating it is refused. It is an integrity check, not a signature: anyone who can edit
the file can recompute it. A line starting with checksum: that belongs to another value is
refused. Every run records the same sha256 in its run store and in every report, so a report
names the authorization record it ran under.
.PP
.nf
version: "1.0"
targets:
  - id: my-chatbot
    base_url: "https://api.example.com/v1/chat/completions"
    endpoints:
      - host: "api.example.com"
        path_prefixes: ["/v1/chat/completions"]
    identities:
      - name: default
        auth_ref: "env://MY_API_KEY"
# checksum: "<sha256 of the body>"   # optional
.fi
.TP
.B targets
The authorized targets (>=1). A target whose id is absent is refused. Each has a base_url,
an endpoints allowlist and >=1 identities.
.TP
.B endpoints
Host plus path_prefixes pairs (default-deny). Plain http is permitted only to loopback
(localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1); everything else must be https. A host with no port authorizes
every port on it; write host:port to pin one, and [::1]:8080 (brackets) for an IPv6 literal.
A request path carrying an encoded slash or backslash (%2f, %5c), a literal backslash, a
double encoding (%25), a ";" path parameter (literal or %3b), a %uXXXX escape, an overlong or
impossible UTF-8 sequence, a segment of dots and spaces only (..., ..%20), or a non-ASCII
character that Unicode normalisation (NFKC) turns into a dot, a slash, a backslash, a percent
sign or a semicolon is refused, since an origin that decodes it may resolve a path outside the
authorized prefix.
.TP
.B identities
Named auth references (auth_ref, e.g. env://NAME); the scope never carries a secret value. For
a multi_identity (cross-tenant) scan, list >=2 identities and set each one's optional
.B canary
to the tenant-scoped marker it owns; authz_leak flags that canary reaching another identity.
.TP
.B commands
Authorized stdio MCP command lines (exact match). Default-deny: a stdio MCP server is launched
only if its command line appears here, mirroring the endpoint allowlist for wire transports.
.PP
Policy-gated specs (the 8 declaring requires_policy: the agentic-extortion suite and PII
elicitation) are gated by the run's policy pack, not the scope. The CLI always applies its own
default pack, which enables no capabilities, and has no option to load another, so these specs
cannot be enabled from the command line and never send (blocked_by_policy with zero sends).
Whether the CLI should accept a pack is an open decision.
.SH "TARGET FILE"
.nf
id: my-chatbot          # must match a target id in the scope's targets
type: chatbot           # model | chatbot | agent | rag | api
provider: openai        # openai (and openai-compatible) | anthropic | mcp | rest
endpoint: "https://api.example.com/v1/chat/completions"
model: "gpt-4o"
auth_ref: "env://MY_API_KEY"
capabilities: { tools: false, rag: false, streaming: true }
sampling_defaults: { temperature: 0.0, top_p: 1.0 }
.fi
.PP
.B id
and
.B type
are required; the rest are needed only for a live scan.
.B auth_ref
accepts only env://NAME; any other scheme (vault:// included: no vault resolver is built) is
refused before anything is sent, --dry-run included. A literal key pasted as the auth_ref is
refused too, and the error prints "a literal value (not shown)" instead of quoting it. The
secret is read at send time and never stored. A provider other than openai, anthropic or mcp routes to the generic REST
adapter. An MCP target sets provider "mcp"; over the wire it gives an endpoint, or as a
local subprocess it sets transport "stdio" and a command argv (authorized via scope commands).
.SH "FLEET FILE"
.nf
version: "1"
targets:
  - id: openai-gpt4o
    endpoint: https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions
    model: gpt-4o
    api_key_env: OPENAI_API_KEY
  - id: local-ollama
    endpoint: http://localhost:11434/v1/chat/completions
    model: llama3.2:1b
  - id: my-mcp
    kind: mcp        # routed to the read-only MCP adapter (discovery)
    endpoint: http://localhost:3000/mcp
.fi
.PP
.B provider
is inferred from the endpoint when omitted: /chat/completions maps to openai, /messages to
anthropic, otherwise rest.
.B api_key_env
names an environment variable, never the key itself.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR dottore (1)
.PP
Templates: specs/scope.example.yaml, specs/targets/example-openai.yaml,
specs/fleet.example.yaml.
